+# Contributing
+
+Thank you for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-small-businesses-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+To keep this list accurate, every submission must meet all of the following:
+
+1. The tool must have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+2. The tool must be relevant to small-business AI use cases such as automation, support, search, analytics, documents, media, or self-hosting.
+3. The tool must not be a proprietary SaaS-only product.
+4. The repository should be active enough to be useful in practice.
+5. The submission must include stars, primary language, license, and the latest visible GitHub activity date.
+
+## Preferred Pull Request Format
+
+Please include:
+
+- Repository name
+- GitHub URL
+- Short explanation of the small-business use case
+- Current stars
+- Primary language
+- License
+- Latest visible commit or updated date from GitHub
+
+## Rejection Criteria
+
+Submissions will usually be rejected if they:
+
+- Link to a product page instead of a GitHub repository
+- Depend entirely on a closed hosted platform
+- Are unrelated to AI use cases for small businesses
+- Duplicate a tool already listed
+
+## Style Guidelines
+
+- Keep descriptions factual and specific.
+- Prefer README-backed wording over marketing copy.
+- Do not add affiliate links, tracking links, or referral codes.
+- Keep categories practical and business-oriented.
+
+## Reporting Issues
+
+If metadata is outdated or a project has changed license, ownership, or status, open an issue with:
+
+- The affected repository URL
+- The incorrect field
+- A GitHub source link showing the correction
+
+## License
+
+By contributing, you agree that your contributions will be licensed under the MIT License for this repository.
